{"tag":"MQ4V","body":"<div class=\"pq-q ue-math\"><p>Let <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"X = [0,1]\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span></span> and let <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Λ\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Λ</span></span></span></span></span> be the set of two orderings of <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"X\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span></span>: the increasing ordering and the decreasing ordering. What is the set of <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Λ\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Λ</span></span></span></span></span>-strictly convex preferences on <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"X\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span></span>?</p></div><div class=\"pq-ans pq-ans--whole\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>The characterization (our answer; the book asks for the set): the <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Λ\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Λ</span></span></span></span></span>-strictly convex preferences on <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"[0,1]\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span> are exactly the strictly convex ones. No continuity is needed.</p></div></div>"}